.include "8clocks.jsim"
.include "nominal.jsim"
.include "stdcell.jsim"

Vclk0 clk0 0 pulse(0,5,2.4ns,.1ns,.1ns,2.4ns)
Vclk05 clk05 0 pulse(0,5,1.2ns,.1ns,.1ns,1.2ns)

*******************************************************************************
* Plug-in your full adder and 32-bit ripple-carry adder subcircuits here.
*******************************************************************************
.subckt fa a b c_in sum c_out
  Xab a b ab_sum xor2
  Xsum ab_sum c_in sum xor2
  Xnab a b nab nand2
  Xnbc b c_in nbc nand2 
  Xnac a c_in nac nand2
  Xcarry nab nbc nac c_out nand3
.ends

.subckt add32 a[31:0] b[31:0] cin0 sum[31:0] cout[31:30]
  Xbit[0:31] a[0:31] b[0:31] cin0 cout[0:30] sum[0:31] cout[0:31] fa
.ends

* 16-bit adder for testing
.subckt add16 a[15:0] b[15:0] cin0 sum[15:0] cout[15:14]
    Xbit[0:15] a[0:15] b[0:15] cin0 cout[0:14] sum[0:15] cout[0:15] fa
.ends

*******************************************************************************
* Your new counter32 subcircuit code should go here.
*******************************************************************************
.subckt counter32 reset a[31:0] f[31:0] clk
  Xadder a[31:0] f[31:0] 0 sum[31:0] unused[31:30] add32
  Xmux[0:31] reset#32 sum[31:0] 0#32 z[31:0] mux2 
  Xdreg[0:31] z[31:0] clk#32 f[31:0] dreg
.ends

*******************************************************************************
* counter32 for testing
*******************************************************************************
.subckt counter16 reset a[15:0] f[15:0] clk
    Xadder a[15:0] f[15:0] 0 sum[15:0] unused[15:14] add16
    Xmux[0:15] reset#16 sum[15:0] 0#16 z[15:0] mux2 
    Xdreg[0:15] z[15:0] clk#16 f[15:0] dreg
.ends

*******************************************************************************
* Pitfall: DON'T use numbers AND iterators in your node names at the same time,
*          e.g., temp[31:0] and temp2[31:0] will result in conflicts!
*
* Also, you may need to use these statements when making your own counter32:
*
* .connect node othernode(s)...
*  = connects the first node to all the subsequent nodes
*    - For example, ".connect a b[31:0]" connects a to b31, b30, ..., b0.
*    - You can also connect nodes directly to ground ("false") by saying
*      ".connect 0 othernode(s)..."
*    - You can also connect nodes directly to vdd ("true") by saying
*      ".connect vdd othernode(s)..."
*
* Xid s d0 d1 z mux2
*  = "mux2" defines a 2-way multiplexer circuit.
*    - If s = 0, node z is set to the value of node d0.
*    - If s = 1, node z is set to the value of node d1.
*    - s, d0, d1 and z are SINGLE BITS. As usual, use iterators to create
*      several multiplexers at once.
*    - Note that the reset node of your counter32 is only a single bit
*      (NOT 32 bits), so to use it with multiple multiplexers, use the form
*      "node#n" to make n copies of the node, e.g., "reset#32".
*
* Xid d clk q dreg
*  = "dreg" defines a flip-flop register q (a single bit of "memory") and sets
*    it to the value of another node, d.
*    - Unlike "connect"-ing nodes, the value only gets stored into q when clk
*      is doing a rising transition (also known as a "positive edge trigger").
*    - Hint: You will also need iteration for this since we need a 32-bit
*      register, which means you will also need to make 32 copies of clk.
*******************************************************************************

*******************************************************************************
* Finally, it's time to simulate.
* This circuit block instantiates the counter32 circuit that you just wrote.
* We pass it a single-bit reset signal, a 32-bit increment value, a 32-bit
* register, and a clock signal (we just used the JSim-predefined clk1).
*******************************************************************************
Xcounter32 reset incr[31:0] reg[31:0] clk1 counter32
Xcounter16 reset incr[15:0] halfcountreg[15:0] clk0 counter16
Xfastcounter32 reset incr[31:0] fastreg[31:0] clk0 counter32

*******************************************************************************
* This "reset" waveform sets up a reset signal that has a delayed start of 15ns
* and then goes to the next value every 50ns.
* The values "1 0 0 0 1 0" indicate that the reset signal only turns on twice
* (at times 15ns and 215ns) for a period of 50ns intervals each, and the signal
* is turned off otherwise.
*******************************************************************************
Wreset reset nrz(0, 5, 50ns, 15ns, 0.001ns, 0.001ns)
+ 1 0 0 0 1 0

*******************************************************************************
* This "incr" waveform sets up a increment value for your counter32 circuit.
* Note that it does NOT have a delayed start. The values "0 1 3 -1" roughly
* translate to the following "commands" in Java-like syntax:
*   reg += 0;  //  0 means don't touch the value
*   reg++;     //  1 means increment by 1
*   reg += 3;  //  3 means increment by 3
*   reg--;     // -1 means decrement by 1
* But note that these "commands" will happen EVERY positive clock edge (and
* only when your reset signal is off)!
*******************************************************************************
Wincr incr[31:0] nrz(0, 5, 50ns, 0ns, 0.001ns, 0.001ns)
+ 0 1 3 -1

*******************************************************************************
* Plot our reset waveform, our incr waveform, clk1, and your 32-bit register.
* Note: sd() displays a range of nodes as a signed decimal (instead of hex)
*******************************************************************************
.tran 300ns
.plot reset
.plot sd(incr[31:0])
.plot clk1
.plot sd(reg[31:0])
.plot clk0
.plot sd(fastreg[31:0])
.plot sd(halfcountreg[15:0])

*******************************************************************************
* ANSWERS TO GUIDE QUESTIONS
*******************************************************************************
* 1. Explain the behavior of the 32-bit register output lines, reg[31:0], as it
*    is incrementing, decrementing, and resetting.
*      - In particular, explain the significant delay between when the 
*        reset/incr[31:0] values change and when the output displays an updated
*        value.
*
*      The reason why there is a delay between reset/incr[31:0] and when the 
*      output updates is because dreg is a positive-edge triggered D flip flop,
*      meaning the flip flop’s output only updates when the clock signal 
*      transitions from 0 to 1. For example, if a slower clock was used in the
*      D flip flop, the time delay between any change to the input and the 
*      output updating accordingly would be higher. Additionally, when the 
*      increment value changes alongside or immediately after the clock’s
*      rising edge (such as the use of nclk1) and its reset is not at 1, the 
*      counter does not update its value with the new increment value until
*      after the next rising edge.
*
*
* 2. What is stopping you from using a super-fast clock to make a super-fast
*    counter?
*    
*      The limiting factor is the propagation delay of the adder. While 
*      incrementing all negative values or all positive values generally pose 
*      no issues, incrementing a negative value to a positive value or vice 
*      versa will result in critical path propagation delay as discussed in 
*      lab 3. More specifically, this is the same case causing the maximum 
*      propagation delay of a 32-bit adder shown in class. Thus, if a clock 
*      with a faster cycle than the propagation delay of 32-bit adder, which 
*      is roughly 4.8ns, is used, then the whole value cannot be fully 
*      incremented before the end of the clock cycle.
*
*      For example, when counter32 is used with clk0, it can be observed that 
*      f[31:0] =  -67108828 when 37 is incremented with -1. In hexadecimal, 
*      -67108828 is 0xFC000024. Coincidentally, 36 in hexadecimal is
*      0x00000024. This highlights that the adder is essentially not done 
*      “processing” the input when it gets stored into dreg[0:31]. In this 
*      example, only the 24 least significant bits are processed to the correct
*      value while the remaining 8 bits are remnants of the incomplete addition 
*      process.
*******************************************************************************
